# EECE 4830 – Phase 2: RDT 2.2 over UDP

This project implements Reliable Data Transfer (RDT 2.2) over an unreliable UDP channel with simulated bit errors.

It supports:
- Sequence numbers (0/1)
- Internet checksum
- ACK-only protocol (NAK-free)
- Stop-and-wait operation
- ACK corruption simulation (Option 2)
- DATA corruption simulation (Option 3)
- Performance experiments and plots

------------------------------------------------------------
REQUIREMENTS
------------------------------------------------------------

• Python 3 installed  
• matplotlib (for plotting)

Install matplotlib if needed:
    python3 -m pip install matplotlib

------------------------------------------------------------
FILES INCLUDED
------------------------------------------------------------

rdt22_sender.py  
rdt22_receiver.py  
rdt22_experiment.py  
input.bmp  (or use your own BMP file)

------------------------------------------------------------
PART A — RUN A SINGLE TRANSFER (2 TERMINALS)
------------------------------------------------------------

OPTION 1: No bit errors (baseline)

Terminal 1 (Receiver):
    python3 rdt22_receiver.py --port 5000 --out output.bmp

Terminal 2 (Sender):
    python3 rdt22_sender.py --host 127.0.0.1 --port 5000 --in input.bmp

DONE when:
• Sender prints: [SENDER] done in ... s
• Receiver prints: [RECEIVER] complete
• output.bmp is created


------------------------------------------------------------
OPTION 2: ACK packet bit-error (corrupt ACK at sender)
------------------------------------------------------------

Terminal 1 (Receiver):
    python3 rdt22_receiver.py --port 5000 --out output.bmp

Terminal 2 (Sender) – example 20% ACK corruption:
    python3 rdt22_sender.py --host 127.0.0.1 --port 5000 --in input.bmp --ack-error 0.20


------------------------------------------------------------
OPTION 3: DATA packet bit-error (corrupt DATA at receiver)
------------------------------------------------------------

Terminal 1 (Receiver) – example 20% DATA corruption:
    python3 rdt22_receiver.py --port 5000 --out output.bmp --data-error 0.20

Terminal 2 (Sender):
    python3 rdt22_sender.py --host 127.0.0.1 --port 5000 --in input.bmp


------------------------------------------------------------
PART B — GENERATE REQUIRED PLOTS (ONE TERMINAL ONLY)
------------------------------------------------------------

This script automatically:
• Starts receiver
• Runs sender
• Measures completion time
• Averages multiple runs
• Saves CSV results
• Generates PNG plot

IMPORTANT: Only use ONE terminal for this section. IF YOU DON'T WANT TO WAIT FOREVER, DECREASE THE SIZE OF THE RUNS WHEN EXECUTING THE LINE OF CODE BELOW

OPTION 1 Plot (baseline):
    python3 rdt22_experiment.py --in input.bmp --out output.bmp --option 1 --runs 5 --port 5000

OPTION 2 Plot (ACK corruption):
    python3 rdt22_experiment.py --in input.bmp --out output.bmp --option 2 --runs 5 --port 5000

OPTION 3 Plot (DATA corruption):
    python3 rdt22_experiment.py --in input.bmp --out output.bmp --option 3 --runs 5 --port 5000


Output files created:
• results_optionX.csv
• plot_optionX.png

DONE when:
    Wrote results_optionX.csv and plot_optionX.png


------------------------------------------------------------
TROUBLESHOOTING
------------------------------------------------------------

Matplotlib error:
    ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'matplotlib'

Fix:
    python3 -m pip install matplotlib


Port already in use:
Use a different port (example 5001) in ALL commands:
    python3 rdt22_receiver.py --port 5001 --out output.bmp
    python3 rdt22_sender.py --host 127.0.0.1 --port 5001 --in input.bmp


Runs taking too long at high error rates:
The experiment script has a 10-second timeout per run.
Timed-out runs are recorded as TIMEOUT and skipped in the plot.

